  "summary": "A monumental royal square complex showcasing Safavid urban planning and architectural grandeur.",
  "description": "Characterized by a vast rectangular plaza surrounded by monumental structures including a mosque, palace, and bazaar, creating a unified urban space. Commissioned by Shah Abbas I to demonstrate Safavid power and Persian architectural achievements, with each building designed to complement the others while serving distinct functions.",
  "culturalContext": "Naqsh-e Jahan (Imam) Square was laid out under Shah Abbas I between 1598 and 1629 as the heart of his new Isfahan — a royal promenade linking the Shah Mosque, Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque, Ali Qapu palace and the bazaar portal. Measuring 160 by 560 metres, it staged polo, processions and commerce at Safavid scale, and UNESCO inscribed it in 1979 as the paramount ensemble of Persian urban planning, its architecture still defining Iranian civic space.",
